'''''Gypsophila paniculata''''', the '''baby's breath''', '''common gypsophila''' or '''panicled baby's-breath''', is a [[species]] of [[flowering plant]] in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Caryophyllaceae]], [[native plant|native]] to central and eastern Europe. It is an [[herbaceous plant|herbaceous]] [[perennial plant|perennial]] growing to {{convert|1.2|m|ft|0|abbr=on}} tall and wide, with mounds of branching stems covered in clouds of tiny white flowers in summer (hence the common name "baby's breath").<ref name=RHSAZ>{{cite book|title=RHS A-Z encyclopedia of garden plants|year=2008|publisher=Dorling Kindersley|location=United Kingdom|isbn=978-1405332965|page=1136}}</ref> Another possible source of this name is its scent, which has been described as sour milk, like a baby's "spit-up".<ref>{{Cite web |last=sghamper |date=2019-08-24 |title=Does Baby's Breath Flowers Smell? |url=https://thatflowershop.co/does-babys-breath-flowers-smell/ |access-date=2023-02-16 |website=#1 That Flower Shop Online |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Why is it called baby's breath flowers? |url=https://www.calendar-canada.ca/faq/why-is-it-called-babys-breath-flowers |access-date=2023-02-16 |website=www.calendar-canada.ca}}</ref> Its natural habitat is on the [[Steppes]] in dry, sandy and stony places, often on [[calcareous]] soils (''gypsophila'' = "chalk-loving"). Specimens of this plant were first sent to [[Carl Linnaeus|Linnaeus]] from St. Petersburg by the Swiss-Russian botanist [[Johann Amman]].

==Cultivation==
It is a popular ornamental garden subject, and thrives in well-drained alkaline to neutral soils in full sun. Numerous [[cultivars]] have been selected, of which <!--AGM withdrawn 'Bristol Fairy'<ref>{{cite web |url=http://apps.rhs.org.uk/plantselector/plant?plantid=891 | title=''Gypsophila paniculata'' 'Bristol Fairy' | publisher=RHS | access-date=2018-08-19}}</ref> --> 'Rosenschleier'<ref>{{cite web |  url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/114520/Gypsophila-Rosenschleier-(d)/Details | title=''Gypsophila'' 'Rosenschleier'  | publisher=RHS | access-date=2020-02-21}}</ref> (with pale pink double flowers) has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref>{{cite web  | url= https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/pdfs/agm-lists/agm-ornamentals.pdf | title = AGM Plants - Ornamental | date = July 2017 | page = 43 | publisher = Royal Horticultural Society | access-date = 3 March 2018}}</ref>

== Toxicity ==
''Gypsophila paniculata'' is toxic to humans, and can cause contact dermatitis.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Gypsophila paniculata (Baby's Breath) {{!}} North Carolina Extension Gardener Plant Toolbox |url=https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/gypsophila-paniculata/ |access-date=2026-02-26 |website=plants.ces.ncsu.edu}}</ref>

==Floristry==
''Gypsophila paniculata'' is much used in the [[floristry]] trade (where it is often simply called "gyp") providing an effective backdrop for larger or more structured blooms. It is commercially cultivated in Peru, forming a major portion of that country's flower exports.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.mapa.es/ministerio/pags/biblioteca/revistas/pdf_hortint/hortint_1995_10_101_104.pdf|title=La floricultura en el Perú: La más alta calidad en Gypsphila|publisher=}}{{Dead link|date=July 2024 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> It is commonly grown and sold for [[corsage#Homecoming and prom|corsages]] in the United States.<ref name="Ruiz">{{cite book | title=The growing problem of invasive species : joint oversight hearing before the Subcommittee on Fisheries Conservation, Wildlife, and Oceans joint with the Subcommittee on National Parks, Recreation, and Public Lands of the Committee on Resources, U.S. House of Representatives, One Hundred Eighth Congress, first session, Tuesday, April 29, 2003 | publisher=[[U.S. G.P.O.]] | publication-place=Washington | year=2003 | isbn=978-0-16-070777-3 | oclc=53336784 | page=97}}</ref>

==Invasive==
''Gypsophila paniculata'' is now widely distributed in North America.<ref>[https://plants.usda.gov/java/profile?symbol=GYPA PLANTS Profile - Gypsophila paniculata L. - baby's breath], PLANTS Database, U.S. Department of Agriculture. Retrieved 6 July 2010.</ref> It is classed as an invasive species in places around the [[Great Lakes]], such as the [[Sleeping Bear Dunes National Lakeshore]]<ref>[http://www.nature.org/wherewework/northamerica/states/michigan/science/art23902.html Does removal of Baby’s Breath from Lake Michigan sand dunes restore native plant diversity and ecosystem function?] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081202114808/http://www.nature.org/wherewework/northamerica/states/michigan/science/art23902.html |date=2008-12-02 }}, The Nature Conservancy. Retrieved 6 July 2010.</ref> and the [[Chicago]] region,<ref>[http://www.chicagobotanic.org/research/conservation/invasive/chicago/index.php Invasive Plants in the Chicago Region] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220120062404/https://www.chicagobotanic.org/research/conservation/invasive/chicago/index.php |date=2022-01-20 }}, Chicago Botanic Garden.</ref> in the [[Pacific Northwest]],<ref>[http://depts.washington.edu/waipc/pnwnoxiousweedlist.shtml Pacific Northwest Noxious Weed List], Pacific Northwest Invasive Plant Council. Retrieved 6 July 2010.</ref> and [[California]] where it is a declared [[noxious weed]].<ref name="Ruiz" />

==Pests and diseases==
Root development is completely inhibited by [[Pantoea agglomerans pv. glysophilae|''Pantoea agglomerans'' pv. ''glysophilae'']]. Both ''Pag'' and [[Pantoea agglomerans pv. betae|''Pantoea agglomerans'' pv. ''betae'']] cause [[gall]] formation. ''Pag'' is a problem for the [[floral industry]], for example in the Israeli industry.<ref name="Barash-2014">{{cite journal | last=Barash | first=Isaac | title=How Way Leads on to Way | journal=[[Annual Review of Phytopathology]] | publisher=[[Annual Reviews (publisher)|Annual Reviews]] | volume=52 | issue=1 | date=2014-08-04 | issn=0066-4286 | doi=10.1146/annurev-phyto-102313-045953 | pages=1–17| pmid=25090476 | doi-access=free }}</ref><ref name="Barash-Manulis-Sasson-2009">{{cite journal | last1=Barash | first1=Isaac | last2=Manulis-Sasson | first2=Shulamit | title=Recent Evolution of Bacterial Pathogens: The Gall-Forming ''Pantoea agglomerans'' Case | journal=[[Annual Review of Phytopathology]] | publisher=[[Annual Reviews (publisher)|Annual Reviews]] | volume=47 | issue=1 | year=2009 | issn=0066-4286 | doi=10.1146/annurev-phyto-080508-081803 | pages=133–152| pmid=19400643 }}</ref>

